{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>And He sought to kill him.</b> It says <b>and He sought</b> — perhaps because, since he was an emissary of a mitzvah, he had a protector, on the principle of Amram, of blessed memory (Pesachim 8b), that emissaries of a mitzvah are not harmed. And the mitzvah in which he was engaged acquired for him one advocate — namely, the one who accompanied him and guarded him and kept the destroyer from approaching him. And perhaps to this the verse intends by saying <b>on the way, at the lodging-place</b> — to say that he was not engaged in the journey of the mitzvah but rather at the lodging-place, and therefore He sought to kill him. Even so, he had a protector, because after all he is reckoned as an emissary of a mitzvah even at that time, and in the interim — while the angels were gathered together — Zipporah acted wisely and removed the stumbling-block; but while he was still engaged in the mitzvah, it is obvious that no destroyer could approach him."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:24:34.672811255Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}